417] [17592186244019 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244019 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5}+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244019 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3056b610-3deb-5e11-aff8-96abed8c674b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244019 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{5}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{5}+b$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{5}+b) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{5}+(kb),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107112" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244021 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C={[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $C$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $C$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f27a4a5-4686-5577-a411-11aa97fab74a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244021 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}]$ is an element of $C$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}ka & 0 \\\\ 0 & -ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $C$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244023 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $D$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of all its entries equals $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72b2f218-4463-5051-8c3b-5d02162b5905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244023 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {d}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {d}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D.$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & {d}_{1}+{d}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})+({d}_{1}+{d}_{2}) & = \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1})+({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1}) & = \\\\ 0+0 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ also belongs to $D.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\in D$ and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ \\alpha c & \\alpha d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and so\nthe sum of the entries of $\\alpha A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a+\\alpha b+\\alpha c+\\alpha d & =\\alpha (a+b+c+d) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $D$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326988 #edb/ref 1080 "I and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326988 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326988 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06326135-466a-5cea-9046-55b5cffec1fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326992 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326992 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326992 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0760e94e-974d-514d-b587-d7fd3c885c53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327041 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327041 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327041 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c8b6873-3df2-5179-b9b5-b5510077e1df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327244 #edb/ref 1080 "II and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327244 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327244 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "23bc63bc-10b5-5f01-890c-5ba8486b87a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327499 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327499 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327499 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40442251-6314-57df-aaca-6cc53942fffb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328037 #edb/ref 1080 "I and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328037 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328037 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7ff1cc31-fa85-5022-b16d-f926a1325d60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328305 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328305 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328305 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9ecdfcc7-8411-5dbf-bf67-8ea5ca0fb879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328326 #edb/ref 1080 "IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328326 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328326 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a0af5a87-cb9e-55bd-95e4-a427c9a8c027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328336 #edb/ref 1080 "IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328336 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328336 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a160159b-6c35-530a-9ad8-654a2c5b6853" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329127 #edb/ref 1080 "II and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329127 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329127 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa6a79d6-167b-5ea4-984e-634f568525dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186326992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329336 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329336 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329336 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186328326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329336 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "53d76603-13d4-5c61-bab7-ac9f12515d55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329512 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186326988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329512 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329512 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329512 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329512 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186329127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329512 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329512 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329512 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186328336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329512 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b97d4e60-e983-5b5a-8733-b469da867546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $L$ containing all invertible $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76fb5f0d-770c-53e0-bb5c-9231d8688147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example if $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=0,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329726 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329726 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43778" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329726 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329726 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $B={p(t)\\in {R}_{3}[t]\\,|\\,p(1)=1}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329726 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd839bdc-19bc-52f4-a042-3d3abcdd708c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329726 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(1)=g(1)=1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+g(1) & =1+1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(1)=1$ and $k=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357759 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357759 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357759 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00a3bcb9-87cf-5e9a-a453-b12b6d936c55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357883 #edb/ref 1080 "